#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1151
msgid "Check digits: IBAN and EIK"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1153
msgid "The shape is half of the check. Both an IBAN and an EIK have **check digits**, computed from the other digits, that catch most typing errors."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1156
msgid "**IBAN (ISO 13616):** move the first four characters to the end, replace each letter by a number (`A` = 10, `B` = 11, ..., `Z` = 35), and read it all as one big number. It must give the remainder **1** when divided by 97. Python's `int` has no size limit, so this is two lines:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1175
msgid "**EIK (BULSTAT), 9 digits:** multiply the first 8 digits by the weights 1, 2, ..., 8, and take the sum modulo 11. If it is 10, use the weights 3, 4, ..., 10 instead; if it is 10 again, the check digit is 0. The result must be the 9th digit."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/08-regular-expressions.md:1192
msgid "(The 13-digit EIK of a branch has a second check digit for the last 4 digits, with the weights 2, 7, 3, 5 - and 4, 9, 5, 7 if the first sum gives 10.)"
msgstr ""

